---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArchitecturePlanes from "../../components/diagrams/ArchitecturePlanes.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="BeaconGuard Architecture"
  description="BeaconGuard architecture across Govern, Authorize, and Verify functional domains: Request Admission, AI provider trust boundary, Result-to-Action Authorization, evidence, and read-only verification."
>
  <section class="doc-architecture">
    <h1>System Architecture</h1>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard is the independent authorization and verification layer for
      regulated AI actions. It separates management of Compliance Playbooks from
      runtime authorization, treats AI-generated results as untrusted proposals,
      and provides BeaconGuard Verify as a read-only investigation surface.
    </p>

    <p>
      Present architecture as three <strong>functional domains</strong>—Govern,
      Authorize, and Verify—not as automatically verified independent network
      planes. Verify is a functional domain; do not assume a separately deployed
      network plane unless a specific deployment evidence supports it.
    </p>

    <ArchitecturePlanes />

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>What this diagram shows</h2>
      <p>
        Govern produces and activates the Active Verified Release outside the
        per-request path. Authorize evaluates Request Admission before provider
        invocation and Result-to-Action Authorization for a declared intended
        enterprise action. The AI provider and the enterprise system of record
        are separate trust boundaries. Evidence feeds BeaconGuard Verify.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Management / control (Govern)</h2>
      <p>
        The BeaconGuard Control Plane manages the governed release lifecycle for
        Compliance Playbooks: environment and integration configuration,
        Integration Policy, System Connector where applicable, Runtime Topology
        and Trust, validation, approval, signing, Controlled Release
        Distribution, and Release Verification and Activation.
      </p>
      <p>
        A distributed release is not automatically active. It must pass release
        verification and activation before it becomes the Active Verified Release
        used by runtime authorization. The Control Plane is not part of the
        per-request execution path.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Runtime authorization domain (Authorize)</h2>
      <p>
        The runtime authorization domain evaluates trusted context against the
        Active Verified Release and returns explicit ALLOW or DENY results.
      </p>

      <h3>Request Admission Authorization</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Request validation and context normalization</li>
        <li>Trust, freshness, and replay checks where configured</li>
        <li>Deterministic Compliance Playbook evaluation</li>
        <li>Verifiable Decision Record persistence</li>
        <li>Fail-closed handling when required controls or trusted context are invalid, missing, stale, replayed, or outside approved bounds</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        ALLOW permits the enterprise application to invoke the AI provider where
        enforcement is configured. DENY hard-stops with no provider invocation.
      </p>

      <h3>AI provider trust boundary</h3>
      <p>
        The AI provider produces the model result. Provider success is not
        enterprise authorization. BeaconGuard does not host the model and does
        not treat a successful provider response as permission to act.
      </p>

      <h3>Returning AI result</h3>
      <p>
        The AI-generated result (including tool or action proposals) is an
        untrusted proposal. Approving a proposal does not resume the original AI
        response. Using the AI response itself as authorization is prohibited and
        not implemented. Semantic response-content governance is not supported.
      </p>

      <h3>Result-to-Action Authorization</h3>
      <p>
        The enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action and
        supplies required context. BeaconGuard authorizes or denies that declared
        action under the Active Verified Release—typically as an independent
        governed authorization with new request identity where required.
      </p>
      <p>
        ALLOW permits the enterprise application to execute the authorized
        action. DENY hard-stops with no enterprise effect. Do not claim identical
        playbooks, identical latency, or separately deployed gateways at both
        authorization points unless specifically verified for a given deployment.
      </p>

      <h3>Governed enterprise system</h3>
      <p>
        The enterprise application executes only authorized actions. The
        enterprise system of record remains authoritative for resulting business
        state. BeaconGuard authorizes; it does not replace the system of record
        or become the business transaction processor.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Evidence provider</h2>
      <p>
        Every deterministic authorization result produces a Verifiable Decision
        Record. Related request and action decisions support investigation of a
        governed AI transaction. Evidence integrity, retention, and
        tamper-resistance depend on the configured evidence provider and
        deployment controls. Append-only or durable-retention claims apply only
        where those controls are explicitly configured and verified.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where enforcement requires it, evidence is recorded before forward or
        before enterprise effect. Mode and configuration determine exact
        evidence-before-effect ordering.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>BeaconGuard Verify</h2>
      <p>
        BeaconGuard Verify is a read-only functional domain for Executive
        Reporting, Decision Explorer, Deterministic Reconstruction, Source
        Evidence and field traceability, Replay Verification, Verifiable Export,
        and AI-Assisted Insights (non-authoritative).
      </p>
      <p>
        Verify cannot modify evidence, change a historical decision, activate a
        release, alter a Compliance Playbook, grant runtime authority, or
        authorize an enterprise action. AI-Assisted Insights must not appear
        inside the trusted reconstruction or runtime authorization path.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Identity, workload identity, and keys</h2>
      <p>
        BeaconGuard integrates with enterprise identity systems while the
        customer identity provider remains the workforce identity authority. The
        IdP does not make the BeaconGuard decision. Architecture reviews typically
        examine:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>enterprise identity integration for workforce access;</li>
        <li>workload and service identity for non-human actors;</li>
        <li>tenant and environment boundaries for authorization context;</li>
        <li>key and secret custody for release signing and evidence verification;</li>
        <li>privileged step-up for sensitive management operations;</li>
        <li>session revocation and lifecycle controls where configured.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Replay, shared state, and monitoring</h2>
      <p>
        Supporting trust dependencies reinforce authorization without becoming
        alternate execution paths:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>replay resistance and shared-state coordination for governed requests where configured;</li>
        <li>immutable authorization context during evaluation;</li>
        <li>evidence-before-effect ordering where enforcement requires it;</li>
        <li>fail-closed shared dependencies when required verification cannot complete;</li>
        <li>operational monitoring of control-plane, runtime, and evidence dependencies.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Investigation Replay Verification in Verify is distinct from admission
        replay protection.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>System of record and customer responsibilities</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>AI Provider:</strong> produces the model result.</li>
        <li><strong>Enterprise Application:</strong> declares intended action, provides required context, executes only authorized actions.</li>
        <li><strong>BeaconGuard:</strong> authorizes or denies under the Active Verified Release; records evidence; provides Verify.</li>
        <li><strong>Enterprise System of Record:</strong> remains authoritative for resulting business state.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer Governance:</strong> retains clinical, legal, operational, compliance, and business judgment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Customer responsibilities also include host hardening, network path
        controls that prevent bypass, transport security, evidence-provider
        operations, and identity-provider integrity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>System Connector for non-native integrations</h2>
      <p>
        An optional System Connector can map source protocols and context into
        the required request format before Request Validation and Context
        Normalization. The connector is optional and non-evaluating: it does not
        evaluate controls, activate releases, or bypass fail-closed enforcement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Trust boundaries</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What enters Authorize:</strong> normalized request or action context and required trust signals.</li>
        <li><strong>What governs evaluation:</strong> the Active Verified Release.</li>
        <li><strong>What BeaconGuard decides:</strong> explicit deterministic ALLOW or DENY at configured authorization points.</li>
        <li><strong>What may reach the AI provider:</strong> only requests that receive admission ALLOW (where enforcement is configured), after evidence requirements are met.</li>
        <li><strong>What may produce enterprise effect:</strong> only declared actions that receive Result-to-Action ALLOW.</li>
        <li><strong>What is prevented:</strong> DENY results and requests or actions that fail required validation, trust, freshness, or replay checks.</li>
        <li><strong>What Verify does:</strong> read-only investigation over canonical evidence; never grants runtime authority.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>Reviewer checkpoints</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Confirm dual authorization points: Request Admission and Result-to-Action.</li>
        <li>Confirm AI results are treated as untrusted proposals.</li>
        <li>Confirm provider success is not equated with enterprise authorization.</li>
        <li>Confirm the AI provider and enterprise system of record are separate boundaries.</li>
        <li>Confirm Verifiable Decision Records for authorization decisions.</li>
        <li>Confirm Verify is read-only and cannot grant runtime authority.</li>
        <li>Confirm AI-assisted insights remain outside the trusted path.</li>
        <li>Confirm the Control Plane is outside the per-request path.</li>
        <li>Confirm the System Connector remains optional and non-evaluating.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-architecture-section">
      <h2>What BeaconGuard is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>BeaconGuard is not the enterprise system of record.</li>
        <li>BeaconGuard is not the business transaction processor.</li>
        <li>BeaconGuard is not the AI or model host.</li>
        <li>BeaconGuard is not a semantic response-content gate.</li>
        <li>The System Connector is not an enforcement bypass.</li>
        <li>Verify is not an enforcement or authorization plane.</li>
        <li>The Control Plane is not part of the per-request execution path.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

